**Proposition 7.3 (the four separate comparison-task kernels).** On
$\Omega_2$, individually for $V=S,B,T,M$,

$$
V(h)=V(h')\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad h\sim_{Q_2^+}h'.
\tag{PVC.8}
$$

Proof. Every cut in this continuing domain has a lawful next occurrence:
at a variable IN use a witnessing canonical completion bit, at a fixed
IN its prescribed bit, otherwise its prescribed row, using ordinary
Advance at an old Ready if Install is not selected. Equal one-step
complete-successor response maps give an equal successful successor.
Its raw state determines the selected memory and extracted data, and
Theorem 4.4 determines the entire successor prefix. Removing its last
occurrence gives $h=h'$. Conversely equal histories have equal domains,
labels, outputs, successors and resource coordinates along every finite
lawful experiment by determinism.

For S, equality gives equal raw fields by the switch/component decoder,
then equal selected M, equal extracted data and equal histories. Hence
it implies task equivalence. Task equivalence gives equal histories by
the preceding argument, and therefore equal support. These prove both
inclusions for S separately.

For B, prefix closure gives equal supports and the same raw/extraction
argument gives equal histories and task equivalence. Conversely task
equivalence gives equal histories and their same terminal antichain.
These prove both inclusions for B separately.

For T, checked erasure of each patch record gives its actual native
occurrence, including supplied bits and creation roles. Equal ordered
words therefore give equal histories and task equivalence. Conversely
task equivalence gives identical histories, so their fixed compilation
and ordered template occurrences are identical. These prove both T
inclusions separately.

For M, Lemma 4.3 and Theorem 4.4 give equality of actual histories from
equal memory structures, hence task equivalence. Conversely task
equivalence gives identical histories and exactly their same selected
backing, frontier and rights. These prove both M inclusions separately.
No intersection argument supplies a missing inclusion. The proof depends
on the complete-successor readout and continuing domain. ∎

